--TEST--
The operator's two removals answer about what they removed and not about what they were asked — a receipt's `delete` is a `bool`, a queue's `purge` is a count, and a second call over rows that are gone answers empty rather than failing — `rule:concurrency/queue-deletion-is-explicit-and-bounded` and `rule:core-classes/queue-storage-is-a-table`
--FILE--
<?nvs
use Core\Queue;
use Core\Queue\State;

// SQLite is the backend a case can really run against, so these two members are
// executed here rather than read: the same removals against the four backends
// with an address are `crates/nvs-stdlib/tests/queue.rs`'s, and what this case
// adds is that a deployment whose jobs live in a file answers the same way.
//
// `workers = 0` is `nvs_config::queue`'s enqueue-only deployment, so every row
// below is in the state this case put it in.

// The two tables, converged the only way a case can: their one home is
// `nvs_stdlib::queue::schema()`, what applies them is the `nvs queue migrate` no
// `.nvst` reaches, and this is the text that command prints for this dialect
// under `--dry-run`.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("jobs");
var $jobs = <<<'SQL'
create table nvs_jobs (
    id integer primary key autoincrement,
    queue varchar(255) not null,
    script text not null,
    args text,
    state smallint not null,
    attempts integer not null,
    max_attempts integer not null,
    backoff_ms bigint not null,
    run_at bigint not null,
    tag varchar(255),
    dedupe_key varchar(255),
    dedupe_pending varchar(255),
    created_at bigint not null,
    claimed_at bigint,
    errors text,
    grants text,
    limits text,
    constraint nvs_jobs_dedupe unique (dedupe_pending)
)
SQL;
$db->execute($jobs, []);

var $dead = <<<'SQL'
create table nvs_dead_jobs (
    id bigint not null,
    queue varchar(255) not null,
    script text not null,
    args text,
    attempts integer not null,
    max_attempts integer not null,
    backoff_ms bigint not null,
    run_at bigint not null,
    tag varchar(255),
    dedupe_key varchar(255),
    created_at bigint not null,
    failed_at bigint not null,
    errors text not null,
    primary key (id)
)
SQL;
$db->execute($dead, []);

var $first = Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs", {queue: "receipts"});
var $second = Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs", {queue: "receipts"});
var $third = Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs", {queue: "receipts"});
echo "pushed ", Queue::stats("receipts")->pending(), "\n";

// One row, named by the receipt that created it. The answer is whether this
// call was the one that removed it, which is why asking twice answers `false`
// the second time rather than throwing at a row that is already gone.
echo "deleted ", Queue::delete($first) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo "again ", Queue::delete($first) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The population, and the retention sweep's own default set: a job still
// waiting for a worker is not a record retention removes, so a queue of nothing
// but pending rows answers `0` and leaves them where they are.
echo "swept ", Queue::purge("receipts"), "\n";
echo "kept ", Queue::stats("receipts")->pending(), "\n";

// The same call once a row has reached a terminal state, and the same call
// again with the state named. Both are counts of what went, which is the answer
// a drain loop reads to know whether to come round again.
echo "cancelled ", Queue::cancel($second) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo "swept ", Queue::purge("receipts"), "\n";
echo "swept ", Queue::purge("receipts", {state: State::Pending}), "\n";
echo "empty ", Queue::purge("receipts"), "\n";

// And the population the two removals left, from the member an operator
// watches.
echo "pending=", Queue::stats("receipts")->pending(), "\n";

// The third receipt is the one `purge` took by state, and this is the other
// half of both members really removing a row: a receipt whose job is in neither
// table is a question that cannot be answered, so the caller is told rather than
// handed a state the row no longer has.
try {
    var $answer = Queue::status($third);
    echo "gone ", $answer == State::Pending ? "pending" : "not pending", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $removed) {
    echo $removed->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `queue.purge` is the grant both removals take, scoped by the queue's name for
# `rule:concurrency/queue-deletion-is-explicit-and-bounded`'s reason: a
# capability answers *may this program remove queue records*, and one row at a
# time in a loop is that same answer at a different rate.
[capabilities.queue]
purge = ["receipts"]

[capabilities.db]
connect = ["jobs"]

[db.jobs]
driver = "sqlite"
path = "jobs.db"

# A deployment that enqueues and runs nothing, so no worker claims a row this
# case is about to remove.
[queue]
connection = "jobs"
workers = 0
--EXPECT--
pushed 3
deleted yes
again no
swept 0
kept 2
cancelled yes
swept 1
swept 1
empty 0
pending=0
Core\Queue::status: no job 3 is in the `receipts` queue on `[db.jobs]`, in either `nvs_jobs` or `nvs_dead_jobs` — a receipt this deployment issued names a row in one of them, so this one was issued by another deployment or its row was deleted
